## 6. Memory Capacity

### 📏 Units

Memory capacity is measured using these units, each one is 1024x bigger than the previous (yes, 1024 not 1000, computers love binary, deal with it):

| Unit | Value |
|---|---|
| 1 Byte | 8 bits |
| 1 Kilobyte (KB) | 1024 Bytes |
| 1 Megabyte (MB) | 1024 KB |
| 1 Gigabyte (GB) | 1024 MB |
| 1 Terabyte (TB) | 1024 GB |

### 🔢 Number of Locations

- Total memory capacity depends directly on **how many memory locations** exist.
- More locations = more storage available, obviously.

### 📐 Word Size

- Also affects capacity — because capacity isn't just "how many locations" but also "how much data fits in EACH location."

### 🧮 Capacity Calculations

This is the formula everyone's scared of but it's actually not that deep:

> **Memory Capacity = Number of Locations × Word Size (per location)**

And remember from before:

> **Number of Locations = 2ⁿ** (where n = number of address lines)

So putting it together:

> **Memory Capacity = 2ⁿ × Word Size**

Example so it actually clicks: if a memory has **10 address lines** and **word size of 8 bits**:
- Number of locations = 2¹⁰ = 1024 locations
- Capacity = 1024 × 8 bits = 8192 bits = 1024 Bytes = **1 KB**

See, not scary at all, just plug and chug 🧃

