## 7. Address Bus and Data Bus

Buses in computing = literal highways 🛣️ that carry stuff between components. Three types matter here:

### 🛤️ Address Bus

- Carries the **address** (the "which location") from CPU to memory.
- **Unidirectional** — travels ONLY one way, from CPU → Memory (because only CPU decides which address to access, memory doesn't send addresses back).
- Width of address bus (number of lines) directly determines the address space (remember 2ⁿ from before? that "n" IS the address bus width).

### 🛣️ Data Bus

- Carries the **actual data** being read or written.
- **Bidirectional** — travels BOTH ways (CPU → Memory during write, Memory → CPU during read), because data needs to flow in both directions depending on the operation.
- Width of data bus determines how much data can be transferred at once (related to word size).

### 🚦 Control Bus

- Carries **control signals** — like "hey this is a READ operation" or "hey this is a WRITE operation," timing signals, etc.
- Basically the traffic signals/instructions telling the other buses HOW and WHEN to do their job.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    CPU["🧠 CPU"] -->|"Address Bus<br/>(unidirectional →)"| MEM["💾 Memory"]
    CPU <-->|"Data Bus<br/>(bidirectional ↔)"| MEM
    CPU -->|"Control Bus<br/>(read/write signals)"| MEM
```

Vibe check to remember: **Address Bus = "WHERE"(one-way), Data Bus = "WHAT" (two-way), Control Bus = "HOW/WHEN" (instructions).**

