## 10. Memory Organization

This is basically the "specs sheet" of how a memory unit is built/structured:

### 🔢 Number of Locations

- Total count of individually addressable storage cells in the memory.
- Directly tied to address lines (2ⁿ, remember?)

### 📐 Word Length

- The size (in bits) of data stored in EACH memory location.
- Determines how much info you get per single read/write operation.

### 🛤️ Address Lines

- The physical lines/wires used to carry the address bits.
- Number of address lines = "n" in our 2ⁿ formula.
- More address lines = bigger address space = can address more locations.

### 🛣️ Data Lines

- The physical lines/wires used to carry the actual data bits.
- Number of data lines usually equals (or relates to) the word length.
- More data lines = more bits transferred per operation = faster data transfer.

```mermaid
mindmap
  root((Memory<br/>Organization))
    Number of Locations
      "= 2^n (n = address lines)"
    Word Length
      "bits per location"
    Address Lines
      "determines address space"
    Data Lines
      "determines transfer width"
```

Quick recap formula everyone loves to test you on:

> **If a memory chip has "n" address lines and "m" data lines:**
> - Number of locations = 2ⁿ
> - Word length = m bits
> - Total capacity = 2ⁿ × m bits

