## 11. Basic Introduction to Memory Hierarchy

Okay last topic, and it's honestly a vibe. Not all memory in a computer is equal — there's a whole **hierarchy** (like a social pyramid, but for storage 💀):

- At the **TOP**: fastest, smallest, most expensive memory — closest to the CPU (Registers, Cache)
- At the **BOTTOM**: slowest, largest, cheapest memory — furthest from CPU (Secondary storage like HDD/SSD)
- **Primary Memory** (RAM, ROM) sits in the middle-ish — faster than secondary, but volatile (RAM loses data on power-off) and smaller in capacity than secondary.
- **Secondary Memory** (Hard Disk, SSD, pen drives) — non-volatile (keeps data even without power), huge capacity, but slower to access.

The whole POINT of this hierarchy is a trade-off: **speed vs cost vs capacity.** You literally cannot have a memory that's super fast, super huge, AND super cheap all at once — pick your battles, computer architecture said "choose two" basically.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    R["⚡ Registers<br/>(fastest, smallest, priciest)"] --> C["🧊 Cache Memory<br/>(very fast, small)"]
    C --> P["🧠 Primary Memory<br/>(RAM/ROM - moderate speed & size)"]
    P --> S["💽 Secondary Memory<br/>(HDD/SSD - slow, huge capacity, cheap)"]
```

Basically as you go DOWN the pyramid: speed ⬇️, capacity ⬆️, cost ⬇️. As you go UP: speed ⬆️, capacity ⬇️, cost ⬆️. It's giving inverse relationship, very mathematically poetic honestly.

