## 🎀 tl;dr for last-minute revision panic

- Memory = stores data/instructions/intermediate & final results for CPU to use
- Two ops only: **Read** (non-destructive, memory→CPU) & **Write** (destructive, CPU→memory)
- Each memory location has a unique **address**; total range = **address space**
- Data hierarchy: **Bit (0/1) → Byte (8 bits) → Word (system-dependent, e.g. 32/64-bit)**
- Capacity = **2ⁿ locations × word size** (n = address lines)
- **Address Bus** = unidirectional (carries "where"), **Data Bus** = bidirectional (carries "what"), **Control Bus** = carries read/write signals
- Read operation: address+control signal sent → memory locates → data sent back via data bus
- Write operation: address+data+control signal sent → memory stores/overwrites
- Organization = Number of locations, Word length, Address lines, Data lines (all connected via 2ⁿ formula)
- Memory Hierarchy = Registers → Cache → Primary (RAM/ROM) → Secondary (HDD/SSD), trade-off between speed/cost/capacity

